<Defs>

  <!--
    Court trade kinds (2026-11-14, decisions D1/D2/D4).

    Why own Defs: TraderKindDef is global, so editing a vanilla kind would change every tribal faction.
    The old wiring pointed the Verdant Court at the vanilla neolithic kinds, and Base_Neolithic_Standard
    carries an unbounded ResourcesRaw generator (Data/Core/Defs/TraderKindDefs/TraderKinds_Base_Neolithic.xml:49-58),
    which sold adamantine, mithril, living wood and elven steel at court settlements - against
    设定/03-派系与对外关系.md:32-34,42.

    Stock shape (D2): every entry is an explicit SingleDef/MultiDef. No Category/Tag/MarketValue
    generator on purpose, so a later item added to one of our thing categories cannot silently join the
    shelf. Adamantine, mithril, the engineering resonance core and the super-rune rubbings are never
    listed (C1/C2).

    Materials: StockGenerator_SingleDef rolls the material of a stuffed item from the stuffs with
    allowedInStuffGeneration (RimWorld/StockGeneratorUtility.cs:44-48, falling back to
    GenStuff.RandomStuffByCommonalityFor), and all four forest stuffs are excluded, so a shelf sword
    would come out of vanilla steel and a steel entropy circlet contradicts its own recipe. The three
    stuffed goods therefore use WhisperOfTheForest.Court.StockGenerator_SingleDefStuff, which takes
    the material from <stuff>: clothing in cloth, war crown, longsword and circlet in elven steel, long staff in living wood (the
    staff's mana, duration and damage read its stuff, CompSpellStaff.cs:33). The short staff, the
    longbow and the terrain-stride mantle declare no stuffCategories, so vanilla makes them with no
    material and they stay plain StockGenerator_SingleDef.

    Quantities are placeholders from 技术/01-种族与派系实现.md:112 until the design gives real numbers.
    Silver is stock, not a sold good: it is the money a trader has to buy from the player.

    The three kinds list the same stock on purpose: the setting only says what the T1-T2 trade carries,
    not that shops differ. They are written out per kind instead of inheriting the generator list,
    because Def inheritance shares list instances between Defs and each TraderKindDef stamps its own
    stock generators in ResolveReferences.
  -->

